        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: a avaria",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "a avaria — \"the breakdown, the fault\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "O carro teve uma avaria a meio da viagem. The car broke down halfway through the trip."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Estar avariado — to be out of order. In Brazil: o defeito, a pane."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "avaria came through Italian avaria from Arabic ʿawāriyya 'damaged goods', built on ʿawār 'fault, flaw' (EtymDB). It began as a word for damage to a ship's cargo."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
